WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily, episode 36.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.260
It's July 18th, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.420
Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.860
Raptor ignition gaps on the latest Starship Test

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.700
forced two engine swaps and reset the timeline

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.860
for proving rapid booster reuse.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.240
Building on yesterday's coverage

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.380
of the Starship Flight Test campaign,

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.380
four Raptor engines failed to reach ignition

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.500
during the countdown for Flight 13.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.120
The failure occurred on the Raptor 3 configuration,

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.620
with the issue isolated to the start sequence transient,

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.600
rather than steady state performance.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.980
Two engines will be replaced before the next attempt,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.920
and SpaceX has not released the precise ignition timing data

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.520
from the static fire logs.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.440
The next flight window opens Monday, July 20th, at 6.45 pm ET,

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.200
with regulatory and range approvals remaining in place

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.020
and no changes to the planned test objectives.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.480
Ground systems perform the planned shutdown sequence

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.140
without incident, and the rescheduled window

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.940
keeps the campaign aligned with proving reliability

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.540
for repeated booster flights.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.320
That proven Falcon cadence depends on multiple paths

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.780
and Starship is now moving to expand

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.260
its own launch footprint.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.340
In 2025, the partly reusable Falcon 9

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.780
flew 165 missions at a 100% success rate,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.140
averaging one launch every 2.2 days.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.340
Booster reuse counts continued to climb

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.400
without reported life limit issues,

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.560
and that demonstrated reliability

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.200
sets the benchmark Starship must reach

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.080
in both flight rate and per flight economics.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.700
Falcon 9's 2025 record already shows how reuse

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.360
amortizes precision components across dozens of missions,

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.500
and Starship must eventually match or exceed that intensity.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.880
As SpaceX pushes its own reuse frontier,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.240
a competing program offers a useful contrast

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.400
in development pace.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.720
Starship hardware is being prepared for transport

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.000
and operations at Florida launch sites

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.640
to open Cape Pads alongside Starbase.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.560
Infrastructure work at the Cape continues

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.500
in parallel with Texas testing,

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.380
expanding available pads and supporting higher flight cadence.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.780
Launch infrastructure readiness at both sites

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.340
remains a key variable in whether Starship

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.280
can achieve the flight rates Falcon 9 demonstrated in 2025.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:32.520
Worth keeping an eye on,

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.580
Russia's proposed reusable medium lift rocket

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.440
remains years behind schedule

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.580
with only suborbital hop tests planned for 2028

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.520
and no orbital hardware yet demonstrated.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.760
The program lacks the integrated engine production

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.820
and launch infrastructure scale

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.460
that enabled Falcon 9's rapid iteration.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.420
Until flight hardware reaches the pad,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.040
the vehicle poses no near-term pressure

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.980
on SpaceX's market position.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.940
And any future Russian flight test campaign

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.980
would still need to close a wide gap

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.060
in demonstrated reuse cadence and production rate.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.380
The gap in demonstrated hardware and operations

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.660
brings us back to the detailed engineering

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.460
behind today's Raptor issue.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.580
Our slash SpaceX users are tracking the exact timing

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.260
of the two Raptor replacements

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.100
and whether a full static fire

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.240
will proceed the July 20 attempt.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.840
Observers note that the ignition gap appears confined

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.300
to the start transient rather than chamber pressure stability.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.380
And several threads compare the current Raptor 3 start sequence

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:39.300
to the earlier Raptor 2 qualification campaign

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.040
that required multiple hardware revisions.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.360
Commenters are watching pad turnaround times

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.500
between the scrub and the next countdown

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.900
as an early indicator of ground operations maturity

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.720
and additional discussion centers on whether

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.460
the two swapped engines will receive individual

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.060
hot-fire acceptance tests before stacking.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.320
On the AI front, no new source developments appeared today

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.080
on the SpaceX XAI GROC cursor thread.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.100
Orbital data center concepts direct to cell backhaul,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.020
colossus power scaling and GROC distribution inside cursor

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.100
remain the active threads to watch.

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:19.320
From an engineering standpoint,

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.640
the Raptor 3 ignition gap that halted flight 13 at T0

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.480
illustrates a classic start transient problem

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.260
in staged combustion engines.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.520
At ignition, the turbopumps must reach self-sustaining speed

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.280
before the main propellant valves open fully.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.320
And any mismatch in spark timing, chamber pressure rise

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:42.220
or gas generator light off creates a window

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.180
where the engine either fails to start

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:47.420
or experiences a hard start that damages hardware.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.320
SpaceX's choice to replace the two affected engines

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.600
rather than attempt an in-place fix

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.220
reflects the priority on preserving the remainder

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.800
of the test objectives and the vehicle stack

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.860
while the data team reviews the precise timing logs.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.240
From first principles, the cost of that decision

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.560
is dominated by the marginal expense

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.960
of two additional Raptor 3 units

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.460
rather than the raw materials inside them.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:15.180
And if each engine's bill of materials

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.740
is roughly one-tenth the assembled price,

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:21.720
the idiot index for a single Raptor sits near 10 times.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.000
Reusing the same engines across multiple flights

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.480
collapses that multiplier by amortizing

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.280
the precision machined components

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.840
over dozens of missions instead of one.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:36.800
And Falcon 9's 2025 record of 165 flights

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:39.220
on reused boosters already shows

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.480
how that amortization works at scale.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.000
The immediate engineering question

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.180
is whether the ignition timing margin can be widened

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.580
through software sequencing or minor valve profile changes

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.960
before the next countdown

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.740
and a successful July 20 flight

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:56.920
would move the program one step closer

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.900
to demonstrating the engine reliability

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.300
needed for rapid booster turnaround.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.760
Continued start sequence issues

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.780
would point to a deeper requirement

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:06.980
for hardware redesign

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.180
or additional ground test campaigns

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.140
before orbital refueling attempts

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:12.800
can be scheduled with confidence.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.160
And observers will watch whether the replacement engines

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.400
undergo a dedicated static fire acceptance test

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.040
or are installed directly.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.060
